telnetu 1.0 for the AT&T UNIX PC (3B1, 7300), UNIX 3.51 with TWG WIN/TCP
==========================================================================

A new telnet client. It has its own terminal, so the telnet hosts of
today (BBSes, MUDs, nethack.alt.org, telehack.com, mapscii.me) look right
on the 3B1. It is /usr/bin/telnetu, with its own Office item: "TelnetU -
VT100/UTF-8 Terminal" (it asks for the host with the form of the Ethernet
telnet item). The old TWG telnet stays /usr/bin/telnet.

In a window (a UNIX System window, or the Office item):
- A window of its own, 80 x 24, with no border, in the 3B1's own font.
- VT100, VT220 and xterm: scroll regions, insert and delete, the
  alternate screen, DEC line drawing, bold, underline, reverse, italic.
  Colours become reverse (light background) or normal.
- UTF-8: box lines, blocks, shades, braille, accents (on capitals too),
  combining marks, Greek and Cyrillic as Latin letters, many symbols.
  Wide letters take 2 cells. A host that sends CP437 (BBS art) is found
  by itself.
- The 3B1 keys: Exit gives the telnet> prompt (Exit again: quit), Help
  lists the commands, Rfrsh draws the screen again, Break sends a telnet
  BRK. Arrows, Page, Home, End, F1-F8 (Shift: F9-F16) go to the host.
  Mouse clicks go to hosts that ask for them.
- A title from the host goes to the window's label (wlabel, wuser).

On a terminal (the serial port, rlogin or telnet into the 3B1, or -t):
- The host's output goes to your terminal as it comes, as with the old
  telnet. The terminal type is your $TERM.
- -a: UTF-8 (and CP437) become plain ASCII, for terminals that do not
  know them: lines become + - |, letters lose their accents.

Both:
- Escape: ^], or ~ at the start of a line (as the old telnet: ~quit,
  and ~~ sends one ~). Then commands as in BSD telnet: open, close, quit,
  status, send (ayt brk ip ao ec el eof nop escape), z (a local shell),
  escape, term, charset, crlf, bs, mode line|char, options. ? lists them.
- Names that are not in /etc/hosts are looked up with DNS: the
  nameserver line of /etc/resolv.conf, else 10.0.2.3 (FreeBee's slirp).
- Telnet options: BINARY, ECHO, SGA, TTYPE (XTERM, then VT100), NAWS
  (80x24), CHARSET (UTF-8), TM. It refuses the others (RFC 1143, no loops).
- A port number on the command line (telnetu host 2323) still negotiates.

Usage: telnetu [-t] [-a] [-e c] [-T type,...] [-C charset] [host [port]]
  -t          plain terminal mode, also in a window
  -a          plain terminal mode (as -t): UTF-8 and CP437 to ASCII
  -e c        escape character (^] if not given)
  -T types    terminal types to give the host, e.g. -T vt100
  -C charset  auto (default), utf8, latin1 or cp437
  -f font     the font file (default /usr/lib/wfont/system.8.ft)
  -V          the version

Build: sh mk3b1 (gcc 2.5.8 in /usr/local/bin, the TWG headers and -lnet).
It makes telnetu. The Office item and its help: ua/Office.add (the lines
at the end of /usr/lib/ua/Office) and ua/telnetu.hlp (/usr/lib/ua).
  telnet.c   telnet protocol, commands, main
  vt.c       the terminal: VT100/xterm control sequences, UTF-8
  glyph.c    letters and symbols into the bitmap
  syms.h     the symbol pictures, made from syms.txt by tools/mksyms.py
  w3b1.c     the 3B1 window (tam, wrastop)
  ttyb.c     plain terminal mode
  net.c      sockets, DNS
Do not copy a new binary over a program that ran before: remove it first
(mk3b1 does). Else the 3B1 can run stale pages of the old one and crash.

Tests: tools/tortured.py is a telnet server that tortures clients
(option loops, UTF-8, CP437, colours, scroll regions, a 100 KB burst).
Run it on the host (python3 tortured.py -p 2323), then on the 3B1:
telnetu 10.0.2.2 2323.
